<DetailLayout title="China Internet & eSIM Guide" subtitle="How to stay connected in China: travel eSIMs, local SIM cards, pocket WiFi, and hotel WiFi explained." description="Complete guide to internet access in China for travelers: travel eSIMs, local SIM cards, pocket WiFi, VPN alternatives, and what to expect from hotel WiFi.">
  <h2>The Situation</h2>
  <p>China's internet is separate from the global internet. Google, Instagram, WhatsApp, Gmail, Facebook, Twitter, and many news sites are blocked. You have three main options for staying connected, and they're not mutually exclusive — the smartest approach is to combine them.</p>

  <h2>Option 1: Travel eSIM (Easiest for Most Travelers)</h2>
  <p>Travel eSIMs from providers like Airalo, Holafly, and Nomad give you data that routes through Hong Kong or Singapore. Because your data doesn't pass through China's network, <strong>all your usual apps work without a VPN</strong>. This is the simplest solution for most travelers — no setup, no VPN configuration, just activate the eSIM before you land and you're online.</p>
  <h3>Cost, Providers, Limits &amp; Requirements</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Cost:</strong> $5-15 for 7-day plans with 1-5 GB of data. Enough for maps, messaging, social media, and light browsing.</li>
    <li><strong>Providers:</strong> Airalo (most reliable, widely available), Holafly (unlimited data plans), Nomad (competitive pricing).</li>
    <li><strong>Limitations:</strong> Data-only — no Chinese phone number. You can't receive SMS verification codes for Chinese apps like Didi or Meituan. Most travelers don't need these.</li>
    <li><strong>Requirements:</strong> Your phone must support eSIM. Most iPhones from XS/XR onward and recent Android flagships do. Check your phone model before buying.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Option 2: Local SIM Card</h2>
  <p>Buy a physical SIM from China Mobile, China Unicom, or China Telecom at the airport or a phone shop. You'll need your passport. Plans are cheap — typically 50-100 RMB for 10-20 GB of data valid for 30 days. A local SIM gives you a Chinese phone number, which is useful for receiving verification codes, using local ride-hailing apps, and making restaurant reservations. <strong>However:</strong> a local SIM routes through China's network, so you'll still need a VPN to access blocked apps. Consider a local SIM for the phone number + an eSIM for unrestricted data as the optimal combo.</p>

  <h2>Option 3: Pocket WiFi</h2>
  <p>Rent a portable WiFi device that you carry with you. Some providers offer "uncensored" pocket WiFi that routes through Hong Kong — these work like travel eSIMs for data. Useful for groups (multiple devices can connect) and for travelers whose phones don't support eSIM. Book online before your trip and pick up at the airport. Cost is typically $5-10 per day. The device needs charging — plan for that.</p>

  <h3>When to Use Each Option</h3>
  <p>eSIM for unrestricted data, local SIM for a phone number, pocket WiFi for groups or non-eSIM phones. They stack — most travelers do eSIM + local SIM and treat hotel WiFi as convenience, not a plan.</p>

  <h2>Hotel & Cafe WiFi</h2>
  <p>Free WiFi is common in hotels and cafes. But it's the Chinese internet — Google, WhatsApp, Instagram etc. won't load without a VPN. Hotel WiFi speeds are generally adequate for browsing and messaging but not reliable for video calls or large file transfers. In Yangshuo's countryside guesthouses, WiFi can be slow or unavailable — don't count on it.</p>

  <div class="mt-8 rounded-2xl border border-stone-200 bg-stone-50 p-6">
    <p class="text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-stone-400">Recommendation</p>
    <p class="mt-2 text-stone-700">For most travelers: eSIM for data (unrestricted, no VPN needed) + VPN app installed as backup for hotel WiFi. If you need a local phone number (for ride-hailing or restaurant bookings), add a local SIM. This combination covers every scenario.</p>
